export const dataAnalyticsText = [
  "PAGE A  /  DATA ANALYTICS & BUSINESS INTELLIGENCE SERVICES",
  "1. Business Intelligence Readiness Assessment",
  "IBCS can help assess an organisation’s readiness for analytics and business intelligence by reviewing business questions, available data, reporting practices, and decision-making needs. The assessment may identify fragmented sources, inconsistent definitions, manual reporting effort, and gaps in data ownership. Findings can be prioritised by business value and feasibility, helping stakeholders focus on useful improvements instead of producing dashboards without a clear purpose.",
  "2. Analytics Strategy and Roadmap",
  "IBCS can support an analytics roadmap that links reporting and analytical work to measurable business objectives. Activities may include stakeholder interviews, use-case prioritisation, data dependency mapping, capability assessment, and delivery sequencing. Each initiative should have a defined owner, intended audience, success measure, and maintenance plan. The roadmap should remain adaptable as business priorities, data availability, and operational needs change.",
  "3. Business Requirements and KPI Definition",
  "IBCS can help translate business questions into clear reporting requirements and key performance indicators. Work may define calculation rules, filters, time periods, target values, exclusions, and accountable owners. Definitions should be reviewed with relevant stakeholders so different teams do not interpret the same metric differently. Documented KPI logic improves consistency across reports and supports more reliable discussions about business performance.",
  "4. Data Source Discovery and Inventory",
  "IBCS can support discovery of approved data sources such as operational systems, databases, spreadsheets, APIs, and business applications. An inventory may record source ownership, refresh frequency, field definitions, access requirements, and known limitations. This helps teams understand where information originates and which dependencies affect reporting. Source access should follow organisational permissions and applicable data-handling requirements.",
  "5. Data Integration for Analytics",
  "IBCS can help plan and implement data integration processes that bring approved information together for analysis and reporting. Work may include source mapping, schema alignment, transformation rules, scheduling, validation, and failure handling. Integration design should account for data volume, update frequency, lineage, and operational support. Clear ownership and monitoring help keep analytical datasets reliable as source systems evolve.",
  "6. ETL and ELT Pipeline Design",
  "IBCS can support the design of Extract, Transform, Load and Extract, Load, Transform workflows based on source systems, data volume, processing needs, and platform capabilities. Planning may define transformation stages, dependency order, error handling, retries, and validation checks. Pipeline design should be documented and tested with representative data, with changes managed so that downstream reports remain consistent.",
  "7. Data Warehouse Architecture",
  "IBCS can help assess and design data warehouse structures for consolidated reporting and analytical workloads. Work may consider source integration, dimensional models, historical data, refresh schedules, access controls, and expected query patterns. The architecture should reflect reporting requirements and operational capacity. Design decisions should document trade-offs between simplicity, flexibility, performance, and maintenance so the warehouse remains useful over time.",
  "8. Data Lake and Lakehouse Planning",
  "IBCS can support planning for data lake or lakehouse environments where varied data formats or analytical workloads justify them. Scope may include ingestion zones, metadata, storage organisation, transformation responsibilities, access controls, and lifecycle rules. The design should define how data is discovered and trusted, and how quality is checked before use. Platform choice should follow actual needs rather than technology preference alone.",
  "9. Data Modelling for Reporting",
  "IBCS can help develop logical and analytical data models that represent business entities, relationships, dimensions, measures, and reporting grain. Models should align with agreed definitions and intended use. Review with business and technical stakeholders can identify ambiguous terms, duplicated logic, or mismatched levels of detail. Clear models make reports easier to build, explain, maintain, and compare across departments.",
  "PAGE B  /  DATA ANALYTICS & BUSINESS INTELLIGENCE SERVICES",
  "10. Dimensional Modelling and Star Schemas",
  "IBCS can support dimensional modelling using fact tables, dimensions, and appropriate measures for common analytical questions. Work may define grain, keys, slowly changing attributes, and aggregation rules. The model should be tested against real reporting scenarios to confirm that users can answer expected questions without misleading totals. Documented modelling choices help maintain consistency as new metrics and source systems are added.",
  "11. Data Cleaning and Standardisation",
  "IBCS can help standardise analytical data by addressing inconsistent formats, missing values, duplicate records, code variations, and invalid entries. Rules should be documented and agreed with data owners, particularly when the correct value is not obvious. Quality checks can track recurring issues and measure improvement. Transformations should preserve source lineage and avoid silently discarding records that may be important to business analysis.",
  "12. Data Quality Assessment",
  "IBCS can support assessment of data quality using agreed dimensions such as completeness, validity, uniqueness, consistency, timeliness, and accuracy where measurable. Checks may be applied during ingestion and before important reports are refreshed. Findings should include examples, affected fields, likely business impact, and accountable owners. Data quality scores are most useful when their calculation and limitations are clearly documented.",
  "13. Master and Reference Data Alignment",
  "IBCS can help align master and reference data used across reports, such as product categories, locations, customer groups, or organisational units. Work may identify competing codes, mapping gaps, and inconsistent naming. Approved mapping rules should be maintained with clear ownership and change history. This helps prevent dashboards from showing different totals simply because source systems classify the same concept differently.",
  "14. Data Governance and Ownership",
  "IBCS can support practical data governance by helping document data owners, stewards, definitions, access rules, quality responsibilities, and escalation routes. Governance should fit the organisation’s operating model and be usable by people who create and consume reports. Clear accountability helps resolve definition disputes, approve appropriate access, and keep important datasets reliable as systems and teams change.",
  "15. Data Lineage and Traceability",
  "IBCS can help document how data moves from source systems through transformations into analytical datasets, metrics, and reports. Lineage records may identify source fields, transformation logic, refresh dependencies, and responsible teams. Traceability supports impact analysis when a source changes and helps explain differences in reported figures. Documentation should be updated when pipelines or business definitions materially change.",
  "16. SQL Analytics and Query Development",
  "IBCS can support SQL development for analytical queries, data validation, aggregation, and reporting datasets. Work may cover joins, window functions, filtering, grouping, parameterisation, and query readability. Queries should be tested against representative data and reviewed for correctness and performance. Shared definitions and version control help reduce inconsistent logic across separate reports and analytical workflows.",
  "17. Statistical Analysis and Descriptive Analytics",
  "IBCS can help analyse historical data using descriptive statistics, distributions, segmentation, and comparisons across relevant time periods or groups. Analysis should state its population, assumptions, and limitations so that results are not overgeneralised. Findings can help stakeholders understand what happened and where variation exists, while avoiding causal claims that the available data and study design cannot support.",
  "18. Diagnostic Analytics and Root-Cause Exploration",
  "IBCS can support diagnostic analysis to investigate why a metric changed or a process produced an unexpected result. Work may compare segments, examine timelines, trace data transformations, and test plausible explanations. Findings should distinguish confirmed evidence from hypotheses and account for changes in data collection or business rules. This approach helps teams target follow-up work rather than relying on intuition alone.",
  "PAGE C  /  DATA ANALYTICS & BUSINESS INTELLIGENCE SERVICES",
  "19. Business Data Exploration and KPI Discovery",
  "For analytics and business intelligence engagements, IBCS can explore operational or business datasets to understand trends, compare segments, examine exceptions, and identify useful measures for reporting. The work may clarify KPI definitions, time periods, aggregation rules, data gaps, and questions that dashboards should answer. Findings should be discussed with relevant business stakeholders before being treated as performance conclusions. This business-oriented exploration supports reporting and decision-making rather than selecting or validating machine-learning features.",
  "20. Trend and Time-Series Analysis",
  "IBCS can support analysis of trends over time, including seasonality, recurring cycles, growth patterns, and changes around relevant events. Work may consider reporting intervals, missing periods, changes in definitions, and appropriate comparison windows. Visualisations should make the time range and units clear. Interpretations should account for external factors and avoid assuming that historical patterns will continue unchanged.",
  "21. Forecasting and Predictive Analytics",
  "IBCS can help evaluate forecasting methods for demand, sales, workload, inventory, service volumes, or other suitable business measures. Work may compare simple baselines with statistical or machine learning approaches and test performance on appropriate holdout periods. Forecasts should communicate uncertainty and known limitations. Their value should be assessed against the decisions they support, not only the accuracy score.",
  "22. Prescriptive and Scenario Analysis",
  "IBCS can support scenario analysis that compares possible outcomes under stated assumptions, constraints, or alternative inputs. Depending on the use case, this may include sensitivity analysis, resource allocation, or optimisation techniques. Results should identify assumptions and trade-offs clearly so decision-makers can understand how conclusions change when inputs change. Analytical recommendations should support, not obscure, accountable human judgement.",
  "23. Interactive Dashboard Design",
  "IBCS can help design interactive dashboards that present relevant metrics, trends, and comparisons for a defined audience. Design may include filters, drill-downs, navigation, contextual notes, and clear visual hierarchy. Dashboards should answer specific questions without overwhelming users with unnecessary elements. Interactions and calculations should be tested with representative users to ensure that displayed values remain understandable and consistent.",
  "24. Executive and Management Dashboards",
  "IBCS can support executive dashboards that summarise business performance using agreed indicators, targets, trends, and exceptions. The design should make the reporting period, metric definitions, and important caveats easy to understand. High-level views may link to more detailed analysis when appropriate. Executive reporting should prioritise decision-relevant information and avoid implying certainty where the underlying data is incomplete or delayed.",
  "25. Operational and Departmental Reporting",
  "IBCS can help create operational reports for teams such as finance, sales, support, logistics, human resources, or service delivery where authorised data is available. Requirements should reflect the decisions each team makes and the frequency at which information is needed. Shared metric definitions help maintain consistency, while role-appropriate detail ensures that reports remain useful without exposing unnecessary information.",
  "26. Self-Service Business Intelligence",
  "IBCS can support self-service BI environments that allow authorised users to explore approved datasets and build their own reports. Planning may include curated data models, metric definitions, workspace permissions, training, and usage guidance. Guardrails should reduce duplicated or misleading calculations while preserving flexibility. Adoption should be evaluated through usefulness and data confidence, not simply the number of dashboards created.",
  "27. Data Visualisation and Chart Selection",
  "IBCS can help select visual forms that match the analytical question, such as comparisons, distributions, relationships, trends, or composition. Charts should use clear labels, appropriate scales, consistent units, and accessible colour choices. Visual emphasis should not distort the data or conceal meaningful variation. Review with intended users can help ensure that a visual communicates the finding accurately and efficiently.",
  "PAGE D  /  DATA ANALYTICS & BUSINESS INTELLIGENCE SERVICES",
  "28. Report Automation and Scheduled Delivery",
  "IBCS can support automated report generation and scheduled delivery using approved BI platforms, data pipelines, or reporting tools. Work may define refresh timing, recipient permissions, failure notifications, and report versioning. Automation should include validation so that stale or incomplete data is not presented as current. Distribution methods must follow organisational access rules and protect confidential business information.",
  "29. Data Refresh and Pipeline Monitoring",
  "IBCS can help monitor analytical data refreshes and pipelines for completion, duration, data volume, validation failures, and source availability. Alert rules should identify actionable problems and route them to accountable teams. Monitoring records can help distinguish delayed source data from transformation failures or dashboard issues. Coverage should be reviewed after changes so new dependencies are not overlooked.",
  "30. Business Metric Certification",
  "IBCS can support processes for identifying trusted or certified business metrics, including their definitions, owners, calculation logic, refresh schedule, and approved uses. Certification should follow the organisation’s own review and approval criteria. Clear labels can help users distinguish governed metrics from exploratory calculations, reducing confusion when reports from different teams are compared or consolidated.",
  "31. KPI Target and Variance Analysis",
  "IBCS can help compare actual performance with agreed targets, budgets, thresholds, or prior periods. Analysis may calculate absolute and percentage variance, identify significant contributors, and show changes by business segment. Definitions and comparison periods should be consistent and documented. Reports should explain material exceptions without assuming that every deviation is negative or that correlation establishes a cause.",
  "32. Cohort and Segmentation Analysis",
  "IBCS can support cohort and segmentation analysis to compare groups defined by a relevant event, time period, behaviour, or business attribute. Work may include cohort construction, retention or conversion measures, and comparisons over time. Segment definitions should be stable and understandable, while privacy and minimum-group considerations should be applied where appropriate. Findings should be interpreted within the limits of the underlying sample.",
  "33. Customer and User Behaviour Analytics",
  "IBCS can help analyse authorised customer or user interaction data to understand journeys, engagement, conversion, retention, and friction points. The approach should use appropriate permissions and data minimisation, with clear definitions for sessions and events. Results can identify patterns for further investigation, but behavioural associations should not be treated as proof of individual intent or used beyond the approved purpose.",
  "34. Sales and Revenue Analytics",
  "IBCS can support sales and revenue reporting by consolidating approved transaction, pipeline, product, region, and time-period data. Analysis may include attainment, growth, conversion, average order value, and forecast comparison where the source data supports these measures. Definitions should align with finance and sales stakeholders. Reports should clearly distinguish booked, recognised, projected, and other revenue concepts to avoid misleading comparisons.",
  "35. Financial and Cost Analytics",
  "IBCS can help organise financial and cost analytics using approved budgets, actuals, allocations, and cost-centre information. Work may include variance analysis, trend reporting, unit-cost measures, and scenario comparisons. Calculations should align with finance-approved definitions and periods, with assumptions documented. Analytical outputs can support review and planning but should not replace formal accounting controls or authorised financial judgement.",
  "36. Inventory and Supply Chain Analytics",
  "IBCS can support analysis of inventory levels, movement, demand, lead times, supplier performance, and fulfilment metrics where data is available. Work may identify stock patterns, delays, or discrepancies requiring operational review. Results should account for timing differences and the reliability of source records. Recommendations should be evaluated with supply-chain stakeholders before changes are made to purchasing or replenishment practices.",
  "PAGE E  /  DATA ANALYTICS & BUSINESS INTELLIGENCE SERVICES",
  "37. Service Desk and Support Analytics",
  "IBCS can help analyse service desk data such as ticket volume, categories, response times, resolution times, reopen rates, and backlog trends. Reporting should define when each measure starts and stops, and account for priority or service differences. Trends can inform staffing and process improvements, while avoiding simplistic comparisons that ignore ticket complexity, working hours, or changes in classification.",
  "38. Workforce and Resource Analytics",
  "IBCS can support authorised workforce and resource reporting on capacity, workload distribution, scheduling, training, or service coverage. Analysis should use appropriate aggregation, access controls, and approved purposes, particularly where employee information is involved. Measures need clear definitions and context so they are not used as unsupported proxies for individual performance. Responsible stakeholders should review sensitive interpretations before decisions are made.",
  "39. Geospatial and Location Analytics",
  "IBCS can help analyse approved location-based data for service coverage, delivery patterns, regional demand, or facility planning. Work may include geographic aggregation, mapping, distance measures, and comparison by area. Results depend on coordinate quality, boundary definitions, and the granularity of available data. Privacy and re-identification risks should be considered when mapping information linked to individuals or small groups.",
  "40. Benchmarking and Comparative Analysis",
  "IBCS can support comparisons across teams, products, regions, periods, or business units using consistent and approved definitions. Benchmarking should account for differences in size, mix, operating conditions, and data coverage. Reports should explain the comparison basis and avoid presenting unlike entities as directly equivalent. Where external benchmarks are used, their source, date, and limitations should be documented.",
  "41. Data Reconciliation and Exception Reporting",
  "IBCS can help compare datasets from different systems to identify mismatched totals, missing records, duplicate entries, or inconsistent status values. Reconciliation rules should define keys, tolerances, timing windows, and exception ownership. Reports should provide enough detail for authorised teams to investigate without exposing unnecessary sensitive data. Corrections should follow approved business processes rather than being applied automatically to ambiguous cases.",
  "42. Data Anomaly and Outlier Reporting",
  "IBCS can support analytical checks that flag unusual values, sudden changes, or patterns that differ from expected ranges. Methods may include thresholds, statistical rules, or suitable models, depending on the data and use case. Alerts should be treated as prompts for investigation, not proof of error or misconduct. Thresholds should be reviewed to balance missed events against excessive false alarms.",
  "43. Data Storytelling and Insight Communication",
  "IBCS can help turn analytical findings into concise narratives that explain the question, evidence, context, and practical implications. Communication may use selected charts, comparisons, and caveats suited to the audience. Reports should distinguish observed results from interpretation and recommendations. Clear storytelling helps stakeholders understand why a finding matters without overstating what the data can establish.",
  "44. Natural Language Query for BI",
  "IBCS can help evaluate natural-language interfaces that allow authorised users to ask questions of governed analytical datasets. The design should map questions to approved metrics and fields, show relevant filters, and allow users to inspect how results were calculated. Ambiguous questions should trigger clarification rather than an invented answer. Permissions and query validation should remain enforced by the underlying data platform.",
  "45. AI-Assisted Analytics",
  "IBCS can support AI-assisted summarisation, anomaly exploration, or natural-language explanations of approved analytical results. Outputs should be grounded in validated data and distinguish calculations from generated commentary. The solution should provide traceability to source metrics and offer human review for consequential use. Evaluation should test common questions, ambiguous requests, and cases where the correct response is that evidence is insufficient.",
  "PAGE F  /  DATA ANALYTICS & BUSINESS INTELLIGENCE SERVICES",
  "46. Analytics Platform and BI Tool Evaluation",
  "IBCS can help evaluate analytics and BI platforms against requirements for connectivity, modelling, visualisation, governance, collaboration, accessibility, scale, and operating cost. Evaluation may include a structured comparison or proof of concept using representative data. Findings should document licensing and integration dependencies as well as strengths and limitations. Final selection should follow the organisation’s procurement and approval process.",
  "47. Analytics Performance and Cost Optimisation",
  "IBCS can help review report load times, query performance, refresh duration, storage usage, concurrency, and platform costs. Improvements may involve query tuning, data-model refinement, refresh strategy, aggregation, or resource adjustments. Changes should be measured against user needs and data freshness requirements. Optimisation should preserve correct calculations and appropriate access rather than improving speed at the expense of reliability.",
  "48. Analytics Security and Access Control",
  "IBCS can support access design for analytical datasets, reports, workspaces, and exports according to approved roles and data sensitivity. Work may include permission mapping, row- or column-level controls where supported, sharing review, and access testing. Controls should be validated using representative user roles. Data access decisions should remain with authorised owners and follow the organisation’s privacy and security requirements.",
  "49. Data Privacy and Responsible Reporting",
  "IBCS can help review privacy risks in analytics, including data minimisation, aggregation, access, retention, and the risk of exposing identifiable information through small groups or detailed filters. Reporting should use authorised data for defined purposes. Privacy measures should be reviewed with responsible stakeholders, and aggregation should not be assumed to eliminate every re-identification risk.",
  "50. Analytics Documentation and Knowledge Transfer",
  "IBCS can help document data sources, metric definitions, transformations, report logic, refresh schedules, access rules, and known limitations. Knowledge-transfer sessions can explain how reports should be interpreted and maintained. Documentation should reflect the deployed environment and identify owners for future updates, reducing dependence on undocumented knowledge and helping users distinguish certified measures from exploratory analysis.",
  "51. Analytics Quality Assurance and Acceptance",
  "IBCS can support quality checks for dashboards, reports, and analytical datasets before release. Validation may compare totals with trusted sources, test filters and calculations, review refresh behaviour, and check access permissions. Acceptance criteria should be agreed in advance, and defects recorded with clear ownership. Testing should include representative scenarios so that a visually polished report is also accurate and fit for purpose.",
  "52. Analytics Incident and Defect Management",
  "IBCS can help investigate analytics issues such as incorrect figures, missing records, failed refreshes, slow reports, or broken filters. Troubleshooting may trace source changes, transformation logic, model relationships, and platform status. Findings should distinguish confirmed causes from hypotheses and record corrective actions. A consistent process helps prevent repeated manual fixes and improves confidence in published reporting.",
  "53. Analytics Governance and Change Management",
  "IBCS can support governance for changes to datasets, metric definitions, dashboards, and reporting pipelines. A controlled process may include impact assessment, stakeholder review, version history, testing, approval, and release communication. Changes to shared measures should be coordinated across dependent reports. Clear governance reduces unexpected differences and helps users understand when a figure or report definition has changed.",
  "54. Analytics Service Handover and Roadmap",
  "IBCS can help prepare analytics services for ongoing operation through documented ownership, refresh monitoring, access administration, support procedures, known limitations, and improvement priorities. A roadmap may identify future data sources, reporting needs, quality improvements, and platform changes. Handover criteria should be agreed with the receiving team, and outstanding issues assigned before the service is treated as operationally ready."
];

